{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>For he was going.</b> That his going on the way was not like one whom others lead, as in וַיָּקָם וַיֵּלֶךְ אַחֲרֶיהָ (II Kings 4:30), but he was going of his own accord, as one taking initiative against the will of the blessed El — for they did not come to summon him for counsel at all." "<b>As an adversary to him.</b> The notion of שִׂטְנָה is opposition to the action of some agent, as in וַיָּרִיבוּ גַם עָלֶיהָ וַיִּקְרָא שְׁמָהּ שִׂטְנָה (Genesis 26:21). Now the angel went forth to oppose Balaam, so that his way would not be right in his own eyes — perhaps he would resort to divination as is his habit and not force the moment — and this in order that he not sin and be lost." "<b>And he was riding on his she-ass and his two lads were with him.</b> And therefore he did not see the angel, as the Sages, of blessed memory, stated (Berakhot, chapter 101): three do not see and are not harmed."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "The Talmudic citation 'chapter 101' (פרק קמא) does not correspond to any chapter of Berakhot, which has only nine chapters. This may reflect a variant numbering or a copyist error; rendered as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:40:46.421436368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}